  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating and preferring the lottery with greater right-skewness (more probability on unfavorable outcomes). Lottery B is more right-skewed and would be chosen by the reason. The individual does consider aspects that are correlated with skewness (higher reward with moderate probability, acceptance of zero), but their reasoning focuses on the appeal of the high reward and their risk tolerance, not on the probabilistic structure or imbalance between favorable and unfavorable outcomes. They do not reference or imply an analysis of skewness as a distributional property. While the final choice might align with the reason (possibly choosing B), the justification is based on different considerations—reward magnitude and risk acceptance—rather than skewness per se. Therefore, there is no clear evidence that the individual used skewness as a decision rule.",
